diff --git a/mac-manual/content/10-boot.md b/mac-manual/content/10-boot.md
index c9d83ad..6cc533a 100644
--- a/mac-manual/content/10-boot.md
+++ b/mac-manual/content/10-boot.md
@@ -54,3 +54,5 @@ Conversion stages, command exit status and non-progress diagnostics are retained
 ## Boot verification
 
 After an update that touches the boot path, `omarchy-mac-boot` checks the kernel, the initramfs hooks, the device trees, the m1n1 payload and the Limine entries against what the installed packages say they should be. A mismatch stops the update before it offers a reboot. Hand-editing any of those files makes the next check fail, which is the point.
+
+On a Mac whose `update-m1n1` is Aurora's, which builds m1n1 stage 2 with its `bootchain.py`, the check asks `bootchain.py` for the image it would build and compares that with the ESP: `m1n1/boot-ctxr.bin` on an M4 or later, whose aura stage 1 is a Mach-O object, else `m1n1/boot.bin`. Aurora tries a changed stage 2 once before it replaces the working one. An update that leaves such a trial armed, of exactly the image the check expects, passes, and so does the trial's own boot until it is committed. A trial that fell back, is stale or was left behind without its marker is reported, and `M1N1_UPDATE_DISABLED` in `/etc/default/update-m1n1` fails the check there, since it also stops Aurora committing or rolling back a trial.
diff --git a/omarchy-mac-boot/README.md b/omarchy-mac-boot/README.md
index 9ce6214..c284af3 100644
--- a/omarchy-mac-boot/README.md
+++ b/omarchy-mac-boot/README.md
@@ -24,6 +24,12 @@ A package can add a device tree node that the kernel's device trees do not have
 
 `omarchy-apple-silicon-boot-check` applies the same overlays when it rebuilds `boot.bin` for its comparison. Each overlay must belong to a package, or the check fails. It reads `/etc/default/update-m1n1` with `OMARCHY_DTB_OVERLAYS=0`, so reading the configuration builds nothing. Where that file does not call `dtb_overlays_update_m1n1`, as with an edited copy pacman kept over the packaged one's `.pacnew`, or where it is missing, update-m1n1 leaves the overlays out, and so does the check, with a warning. `95-omarchy-mac-dtb-overlays.hook` runs `update-m1n1` when a package adds, changes or removes an overlay, as `95-m1n1-install.hook` does after a kernel update. So the next boot and the boot check read the same device trees. pacman reads its hooks when a transaction starts, so the transaction that first installs this hook does not run it: an overlay installed in that same transaction, with no kernel update beside it, reaches `boot.bin` at the next `update-m1n1` (`sudo update-m1n1`, or the next kernel update), and until then the boot check reports `boot.bin` as stale.
 
+## Aurora's Stage 2
+
+On a Mac whose `/usr/bin/update-m1n1` is Aurora's (its asahi-scripts, which hand the build to `/usr/libexec/aurora/bootchain.py`), `omarchy-apple-silicon-boot-check` does not rebuild `boot.bin` the stock way. `lib/aurora-stage2.sh` reads the wrapper's defaults and `/etc/default/update-m1n1` (with `OMARCHY_DTB_OVERLAYS=0`, as for the stock check), and `lib/aurora-stage2.py` loads `bootchain_arch.py` and asks it, as root with the environment the wrapper would export, for its plan (the step `update-m1n1 --check` runs), how it reads a copy of the `aura-try` marker, and its record of trying a candidate. It never runs `update-m1n1` or bootchain's apply, commit or guard, so nothing on the ESP, in `/boot` or in `/var` changes. The plan gives the image and its file name: `m1n1/boot-ctxr.bin` where aura's monitor-free marker (`/usr/share/aura/monitor-free`) and an M4 or later compatible say Stage 1 is a Mach-O object, with only this board's device tree, else `m1n1/boot.bin` with every Apple device tree. The check compares that with the system ESP, read through the same read-only mount. An `M1N1` set in `/etc/default/update-m1n1` to a file no package installs, such as a pinned aura Stage 2, is followed and reported; a default `M1N1` must belong to the bootloader package.
+
+Where aura supports it, Aurora's update-m1n1 tries a changed Stage 2 once before it replaces the live one: the candidate `.next`, its `.next.json` receipt, a record in `/var/lib/aurora-stage2/attempts` and the `aura-try` marker at the ESP root, which aura marks spent when it boots the candidate and `aurora-stage2-commit.service` removes when it commits it. The check passes a trial of exactly the image it gets from the plan, armed or running this boot (`/proc/device-tree/chosen/aurora,trial`), with a receipt and an attempt record bootchain matches to it, since the commit service requires them. It fails a stale or incomplete trial, an `aura-try` that arms another image, a trial that fell back while the live image is not the current build, and a candidate with no marker beside a stale live image; beside a current live image, leftovers of a fallen-back or orphaned trial are a warning. Where update-m1n1 has already tried the current build, which it never arms again, the check says so instead of advising `sudo update-m1n1`. `M1N1_UPDATE_DISABLED` fails both checks here, not only the full one: Aurora's wrapper then exits before anything, the boot-time commit and guard included. Aurora's files under `test/fixtures/aurora` are asahi-scripts 20261023-9's, unchanged, so the tests run the real plan.
+
 ## Temporary adaptations
 
 - Deploy ARM64 Limine to U-Boot's `EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI` slot until the shared Limine installer supports that target.
